Subject: EXL3 n-gram table: parallel deduplicated reads, read ahead of the GPU wait

Applies to TensorFold 609ca419 (0.6.5) after the recipe's patches/vision-prefix-cache.patch.

Why: on one Spark the EXL3 pack's 36 GiB n-gram table is a memory map. NgramTable.gather read
rows by numpy fancy indexing into that map, so every row not in the page cache was a 4 KiB
page fault, taken one at a time on one thread; and for EXL3 the gather ran after the wait
for the GPU (stage(): `w.x3 is None` excluded it from the read-ahead the MLX path has).
Uncached prompts prefilled at ~450-700 tok/s with the GPU idle on the SSD.

What:
- NgramTable.gather (TF_EXL3_NGRAM_READ=pread, the default): each unique row once, adjacent
  rows coalesced (<= 1 MiB a read), os.preadv on TF_EXL3_NGRAM_READERS threads (default 32),
  files opened with POSIX_FADV_RANDOM. Same bytes as the map (tests). TF_EXL3_NGRAM_READ=mmap
  restores the original path.
- forward.stage: the EXL3 path also reads its rows before b.staged.synchronize(), so the
  reads overlap the previous step's GPU work; stage_ple takes the rows read ahead.
- tests/test_exl3_ngram_pread.py.

